\\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441899 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc39d04c-8fc6-5458-bd77-d57b514616c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441899 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441900 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441900 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441900 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d724ae77-2273-56ab-852e-2415e473e7f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441900 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{2}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
